WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.740
Storage duration describes when the storage for an object exists.

2
00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:16.600
In practical C engineering, it helps answer whether a pointer can remain valid after a function returns, whether cleanup is automatic or explicit, and whether a value can be shared across calls or threads.

3
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:24.700
The language details matter, but the everyday consequence is this: different storage durations create different lifetime contracts.

4
00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:31.380
Automatic storage duration is commonly associated with local objects inside a block or function.

5
00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:35.560
These objects exist only for the execution of that scope.

6
00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:41.340
Their storage is convenient and efficient, but their addresses should not be retained after the scope ends.

7
00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:49.600
Returning or storing a pointer to an automatic object can leave later code with a reference to an object that no longer exists.

8
00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:54.760
Static storage duration means an object can exist for the lifetime of the program.

9
00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:58.860
Global objects and static locals fall into this category.

10
00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:11.200
Their long lifetime can be useful, but it can also hide dependencies, preserve state between calls, complicate testing, and introduce shared mutable state that needs access discipline.

11
00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:16.440
Thread storage duration, where supported, gives an object a lifetime tied to a thread.

12
00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:21.100
That can reduce cross-thread sharing, but it does not remove lifetime questions.

13
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:31.680
Code still needs to know when the thread starts and ends, how initialization occurs, and whether pointers to thread-local objects escape into longer-lived or different-thread contexts.

14
00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:38.200
Allocated storage duration is created by dynamic allocation and ends when the owner releases the storage.

15
00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:44.300
It is flexible because the object's lifetime can be controlled by program logic rather than a block scope.

16
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:49.920
That flexibility requires explicit cleanup responsibility and clear ownership transfer rules.

17
00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:53.080
Storage duration is not the same as validity.

18
00:01:53.180 --> 00:02:02.940
Storage may exist while the object is uninitialized, partially initialized, transferred, logically destroyed, or unavailable for a particular operation.

19
00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:09.760
Safe C code combines storage duration with object state, ownership, and API contracts.
